[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Linux netfilter/iptables policy on router6 (the site-C gateway), specifically the FORWARD chain filtering packets arriving on eth2 (10.50.1.2).

Mechanism: Traffic from Location A to Location C traverses router3 -> router6 via 10.50.1.0/24 (eth2 on router6) and should then be forwarded to 172.18.51.0/24 (eth1). On router6 the FORWARD chain contains blanket drop rules for any traffic with -i eth2, allowing only a very narrow TCP/80 exception. As a result, data-plane packets are discarded at router6 even though RIP control-plane routes remain present and stable. To users this appears as “routes aging out/relearning,” but in reality routes exist while traffic is intermittently black-holed by the firewall.

Localization: router6 FORWARD chain on inbound interface eth2. Evidence:
- pc4 traceroute to 172.18.51.15 reaches 10.50.1.2 (router6) then times out.
- router6 can ping both 172.18.51.15 and 172.16.10.4 (local forwarding not traversing FORWARD drops).
- router3 cannot ping 172.18.51.15 (blocked at router6).
- router6 iptables shows:
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP (plus UDP/TCP drops); counters incrementing.
- RIP tables on all routers are steady; no rapid aging observed.

Root cause: A misconfigured iptables policy on router6 is dropping all forwarded traffic arriving on eth2, except a single TCP/80 exception, breaking inter-site connectivity.

Corrective fix: Remove or adjust the FORWARD chain on router6 to permit inter-site traffic. For example: flush the FORWARD rules (iptables -F FORWARD), then add stateful permit (iptables -A FORWARD -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT) and explicit allows between 10.50.1.0/24 and 172.18.50.0/24,172.18.51.0/24, or simply allow forwarding between eth2 and eth0/eth1 as required. Ensure no blanket -i eth2 DROP remains.
=== END ===